WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.799
Tool failures don't always mean the operation is impossible.

2
00:00:03.799 --> 00:00:08.600
Sometimes a service is temporarily overloaded, or a retry will succeed.

3
00:00:08.600 --> 00:00:12.899
Sometimes a batch job finishes half its items before hitting an error.

4
00:00:12.899 --> 00:00:17.899
In this video, we'll work through retry-safe patterns, partial success responses,

5
00:00:17.899 --> 00:00:21.399
and circuit breakers for unreliable downstream services.

6
00:00:21.399 --> 00:00:23.299
Let's get into it.

7
00:00:23.299 --> 00:00:27.299
A retry sounds harmless until it charges a customer twice.

8
00:00:27.299 --> 00:00:33.799
Partial success describes the outcome when a batch tool completes some operations and fails on others.

9
00:00:33.799 --> 00:00:38.000
The response has to say which items succeeded and which didn't,

10
00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:40.799
so Claude can reason about what to do next.

11
00:00:40.799 --> 00:00:47.099
Idempotency is the property that calling a tool twice with the same input is safe.

12
00:00:47.099 --> 00:00:54.200
It's the foundation of retry logic, because without it, retrying a call can create duplicate side effects.

13
00:00:54.200 --> 00:01:00.900
And the circuit breaker is a pattern that cuts off calls to a failing downstream service after repeated failures,

14
00:01:00.900 --> 00:01:07.300
preventing the kind of cascading errors that fill a context window with noise.

15
00:01:07.300 --> 00:01:12.300
Before you retry, you need to know whether retrying actually makes sense.

16
00:01:12.300 --> 00:01:17.900
Transient errors like timeouts and rate limit responses are the clearest candidates.

17
00:01:17.900 --> 00:01:20.900
The service was busy or temporarily unavailable,

18
00:01:21.000 --> 00:01:25.199
and waiting a short moment before trying again is the right call.

19
00:01:25.199 --> 00:01:27.300
User errors are the opposite.

20
00:01:27.300 --> 00:01:33.099
If the input was invalid, sending the exact same request again won't fix anything.

21
00:01:33.099 --> 00:01:38.000
Claude should surface the problem to the user and ask for a correction instead.

22
00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:44.900
System errors without a suggested action fall somewhere in between and need judgment before retrying.

23
00:01:44.900 --> 00:01:52.699
Your error codes should make the category explicit, so Claude doesn't have to infer it from message text alone.

24
00:01:52.699 --> 00:01:56.500
Idempotency is the key property that makes retry safe.

25
00:01:56.500 --> 00:02:02.400
An idempotent tool returns the same result whether you call it once or ten times with the same input.

26
00:02:02.400 --> 00:02:05.599
A lookup or a read is idempotent by nature.

27
00:02:05.599 --> 00:02:10.699
A write that creates a new record isn't, unless you build idempotency indeliberately.

28
00:02:10.699 --> 00:02:17.300
The standard pattern is for the caller to generate a unique idempotency key and include it with the request.

29
00:02:17.300 --> 00:02:24.600
The tool stores the key, and if it sees the same key again, returns the original response instead of executing again.

30
00:02:24.600 --> 00:02:27.399
Payment APIs use this pattern extensively.

31
00:02:27.399 --> 00:02:32.199
It's worth adopting in any tool that creates or modifies state.

32
00:02:32.199 --> 00:02:35.600
Designing for safe retry starts at the tool interface level.

33
00:02:35.600 --> 00:02:42.300
The most direct approach is accepting a caller-provided idempotency key with every state-changing request.

34
00:02:42.300 --> 00:02:48.800
The tool stores the key alongside the result and returns the cached result for any duplicate call.

35
00:02:48.800 --> 00:02:51.100
Conditional writes are another option.

36
00:02:51.100 --> 00:02:57.600
The tool checks current state before applying a change and skips the write if it's already been applied.

37
00:02:57.600 --> 00:03:05.300
Either approach turns a non-idempotent operation into one Claude can safely retry on transient failures.

38
00:03:05.300 --> 00:03:09.100
The cost is a bit of storage and a lookup on each call.

39
00:03:09.100 --> 00:03:16.061
The benefit is that Claude never creates a duplicate charge, email, or record because of a network

40
00:03:16.061 --> 00:03:19.328
hiccup between the tool call and the response.

41
00:03:19.399 --> 00:03:23.699
Three retry failure modes come up frequently enough to call out by name.

42
00:03:23.699 --> 00:03:29.799
Duplicate side effects happen when a non-idempotent tool gets retried and creates two records,

43
00:03:29.799 --> 00:03:32.135
charges, or messages instead of one.

44
00:03:32.199 --> 00:03:38.399
This is the most expensive failure mode because real-world consequences like double charges are hard to reverse.

45
00:03:38.399 --> 00:03:44.466
Infinite retry loops happen when there's no back-off delay or retry limit, so Claude keeps hammering

46
00:03:44.466 --> 00:03:47.439
a failing service until the context window fills.

47
00:03:47.500 --> 00:03:53.966
And stale state failures happen when a retry reapplies steps that already completed during a partial

48
00:03:53.966 --> 00:03:57.135
run, leaving the system in an inconsistent state.

49
00:03:57.199 --> 00:04:00.100
Each of these is avoidable with the right design choices.

50
00:04:00.100 --> 00:04:02.600
Idempotency keys prevent duplicates.

51
00:04:02.600 --> 00:04:05.100
Back-off and limits prevent loops.

52
00:04:05.100 --> 00:04:09.800
And partial success responses prevent rerunning completed steps.

53
00:04:09.800 --> 00:04:14.800
Partial success is the case where a batch tool completes some items and fails on others.

54
00:04:14.800 --> 00:04:20.000
The wrong response is to return a generic error that hides what succeeded.

55
00:04:20.000 --> 00:04:25.000
The right response uses a partial success status and splits the result into two lists.

56
00:04:25.000 --> 00:04:33.000
A succeeded list with the IDs or keys that completed, and a failed list with per-item error codes and context.

57
00:04:33.000 --> 00:04:40.500
When Claude receives this structure, it can decide what to retry, what to skip, and what to surface to the user.

58
00:04:40.500 --> 00:04:46.577
Without it, Claude either has to guess or rerun the entire batch, which risks duplicating the

59
00:04:46.577 --> 00:04:48.734
operations that already finished.

60
00:04:48.799 --> 00:04:56.200
Surfacing partial success explicitly is what the Architect Foundation's exam tests in this area.

61
00:04:56.200 --> 00:05:02.700
The circuit breaker pattern manages the relationship between your tool and an unreliable downstream service.

62
00:05:02.700 --> 00:05:04.299
It has three states.

63
00:05:04.299 --> 00:05:08.899
In the closed state, calls pass through normally and failures are counted.

64
00:05:08.899 --> 00:05:14.331
Once failures exceed a threshold, the breaker opens and calls are blocked immediately without

65
00:05:14.331 --> 00:05:16.141
hitting the downstream service.

66
00:05:16.200 --> 00:05:21.700
After a configured interval, the breaker moves to half open and allows a single probe call through.

67
00:05:21.700 --> 00:05:26.500
If the probe succeeds, the breaker closes and normal traffic resumes.

68
00:05:26.500 --> 00:05:30.200
If it fails, the breaker stays open and the interval resets.

69
00:05:30.200 --> 00:05:35.890
This pattern prevents Claude from filling its context with errors while waiting for a service that

70
00:05:35.890 --> 00:05:37.341
won't recover on its own.

71
00:05:37.399 --> 00:05:43.899
You should add a circuit breaker when a downstream tool has intermittent outages that aren't predictable.

72
00:05:44.000 --> 00:05:48.100
The configuration has three parameters you need to set deliberately.

73
00:05:48.100 --> 00:05:52.899
The failure threshold is how many consecutive failures trigger the open state.

74
00:05:52.899 --> 00:05:57.000
Set it too low and the breaker trips on normal variance.

75
00:05:57.000 --> 00:06:00.799
Set it too high and you waste calls on a broken service.

76
00:06:00.799 --> 00:06:05.799
The recovery probe interval is how long to wait before testing the service again.

77
00:06:05.799 --> 00:06:09.899
And the fallback defines what to return while the circuit is open.

78
00:06:09.899 --> 00:06:15.899
A cached result, a simplified response, or a clean error that tells Claude to escalate.

79
00:06:15.899 --> 00:06:22.100
Without a fallback, the circuit breaker just converts tool failures into blocked calls.

80
00:06:22.100 --> 00:06:28.399
The types of failures that nobody forgets are things like a retry that charged a customer twice

81
00:06:28.399 --> 00:06:34.200
or the batch that resent every email because the response only said error.

82
00:06:34.200 --> 00:06:37.000
Reliability lives on the tool side.

83
00:06:37.000 --> 00:06:41.500
An idempotency key makes a repeat call harmless.

84
00:06:41.500 --> 00:06:46.000
A succeeded and failed split tells Claude exactly what's left.

85
00:06:46.000 --> 00:06:50.000
And a breaker keeps it from hammering a service that's already down.

86
00:06:50.000 --> 00:06:53.000
That's a wrap on this one. I'll see you in the next lesson.

